In my (/2013/01/how-to-choose-a-degree-for-earning-to-give-and-research/) I looked at the role of degree choice for professional and academic careers. Now let’s branch out and look at the more general role of degree choice. This matters for people interested in (/advocacy), (/innovation), (/improving) as well as (/earning-to-give) in non-professional careers. At this stage in our research, it seems that degrees in more quantitative subjects improve your employment prospects and your flexibility, which is important for making a difference. The next most important thing is to pick a degree you expect to do well in. But, again, we’ll be refining that view as we explore more of the evidence.

